1
00:00:02,360 --> 00:00:05,140
Hi, everyone. My name
is Dr. Joseph Holloway,

2
00:00:05,140 --> 00:00:06,680
and in this session,
we're going to

3
00:00:06,680 --> 00:00:09,000
be discussing inflammatory
heart disease.

4
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:15,780
Now, inflammatory heart
disease takes into account

5
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,360
all the layers of the
heart. So we're going to be

6
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:22,400
discussing endocarditis,
myocarditis, and pericarditis.

7
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:26,740
We'll describe the
etiologies for each one, the

8
00:00:26,740 --> 00:00:29,040
clinical presentation
that you would expect, and

9
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:32,060
how you could diagnose
each one of those, in

10
00:00:32,060 --> 00:00:34,480
addition to mentioning some
of the related clinical

11
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:37,180
forms of inflammatory
heart disease as well.

12
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:42,860
All right, so we're
going to start here

13
00:00:42,860 --> 00:00:45,800
with endocarditis,
which should be a

14
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,100
bit of a review from
a previous lecture.

15
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,880
So endocarditis is the
inflammation of the

16
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:56,360
heart's internal lining,
which is the endocardium.

17
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:59,760
And of course, that takes
into account the valves as

18
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:02,680
well. And the most frequent
valve that's going to be

19
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:05,760
involved and colonized is
going to be the mitral valve

20
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:09,400
on the left side of the heart,
and then that's followed

21
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:12,560
by the aortic valve. Very
infrequently are you going

22
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:15,320
to see valves on the right
side of the heart involved,

23
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,700
like the tricuspid valve,
and that would really

24
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:21,740
only be associated with the
risk factor of IV drug use.

25
00:01:24,930 --> 00:01:27,360
All right, so speaking
of risk factors,

26
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,060
yes, certainly IV drug
use would be a risk factor

27
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,360
for right-sided endocarditis,
specifically associated

28
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:37,100
with the tricuspid
valve. But certainly age

29
00:01:37,100 --> 00:01:40,320
is a big risk factor,
which should make sense

30
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:43,640
because as individuals age,
oftentimes there's more

31
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:46,320
of a need for the insertion
of prosthetic devices.

32
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:47,580
Um,

33
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:50,400
oral manipulation is a
big risk factor, right?

34
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:54,180
And so that would allow
for, um, your normal oral

35
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:57,020
flora, uh, portal of
entry to gain access into

36
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:01,710
your bloodstream and, uh,
any sort of history of,

37
00:02:01,710 --> 00:02:04,520
um, heart issues, right?
So, uh, preexisting

38
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:07,840
infections, any sort of
structural issues or congenital

39
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:11,460
issues, uh, and any
sort of, um, history of

40
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:14,440
surgical procedures that
you have with your heart.

41
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:18,840
All right. Now, what we're
going to do is just kind

42
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:22,820
of touch on and remind you
about the pathogenesis of

43
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:25,560
endocarditis. So there's
basically two things at

44
00:02:25,560 --> 00:02:29,110
play here. Number one, you
need some sort of preexisting

45
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:32,560
injury, endothelial injury,
that's going to allow

46
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:36,760
for the exposure of
subendothelial matrix proteins.

47
00:02:37,020 --> 00:02:39,840
And when you have that,
what's going to occur is

48
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:42,340
going to be the attachment
of serum proteins and

49
00:02:42,340 --> 00:02:45,560
platelets creating a
thrombus in that location.

50
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:50,980
Now, second to that, you
need transient bacteremia,

51
00:02:51,100 --> 00:02:53,180
right? So you need some
sort of microbe in the blood

52
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,880
that then has the ability
to attach to that thrombus.

53
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,660
And so once that occurs,
now you have a population of

54
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:04,040
microbes, generally bacteria,
that are now dividing.

55
00:03:04,260 --> 00:03:07,300
That's going to recruit
even more serum proteins,

56
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:11,580
immune proteins, platelets
to that location, creating

57
00:03:11,580 --> 00:03:14,900
this expanding structure
known as a vegetation. And

58
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:18,360
it's that vegetation then
that has the ability to

59
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:22,800
obviously disrupt the tissue,
destroy the tissue, and

60
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:26,500
also will impair the
functionality of the valve. And

61
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:30,400
that's where you get the
new murmur or the valvular

62
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:34,080
regurgitation that's
associated with endocarditis.

63
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:39,000
And of course, you could
also then have little parts

64
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,020
of that vegetation break off
and become septic emboli.

65
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:45,160
So that can happen on the
right side of the heart

66
00:03:45,160 --> 00:03:48,640
and become a pulmonary
embolism. And it can also happen

67
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,830
on the left side of the
heart. And if that occurs,

68
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,620
basically the embolism
can go basically

69
00:03:54,620 --> 00:03:56,820
anywhere in systemic
circulation.

70
00:04:00,380 --> 00:04:04,420
so what we're going to do
is take a brief look at some

71
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:07,220
of the main groups of
bacteria and microbes that

72
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:10,560
cause ie and of course
you have to start with the

73
00:04:10,560 --> 00:04:15,480
staphylococci so both staph
aureus and staph epidermidis so

74
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:20,480
staph aureus by itself is
responsible for approximately

75
00:04:20,480 --> 00:04:24,020
somewhere in the order
of about 30% of all cases

76
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:28,080
of IE, in addition to
generally being the case that

77
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:32,720
the cause of any acute case
of IE as well. And the reason

78
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:37,180
for that is due to its
virulence factors. So it has

79
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:41,740
the potential uniquely
to be able to bind to non

80
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:45,660
-injured healthy endothelial
cells or healthy heart valves.

81
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:48,420
And it does that using
surface level binding

82
00:04:48,420 --> 00:04:52,180
proteins. So the binding
proteins known as fibronectin

83
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:56,000
binding proteins that can
actually attach to healthy

84
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,440
endothelial cells. And once
that happens, it also uses a

85
00:05:00,440 --> 00:05:03,580
second surface level binding
protein known as clumping

86
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:06,320
factor that could then
recruit serum proteins like

87
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:10,340
fibrin and platelets as
well to be able to start

88
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:15,380
creating a vegetation from
scratch. So that is basically

89
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:18,960
how you can take a healthy
heart valve and start the

90
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:23,080
endocarditis picture using
specifically staph aureus.

91
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,180
Now being able to differentiate
the causative agent

92
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:31,040
of IE one from the other
is going to be important

93
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:34,700
biochemically and the way
that you do that is first with

94
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:37,220
the catalase test. So catalase
is going to be the way

95
00:05:37,220 --> 00:05:39,460
that you differentiate the
staph group from the strep

96
00:05:39,460 --> 00:05:43,020
group and both of those
groups are really the main

97
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:47,360
causative agents of most cases
of IE. So the staph group

98
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:51,040
is going to be catalase
positive, whereas the strep

99
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:54,060
group tends to be catalase
negative. So a really easy

100
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:57,120
way that you can biochemically
differentiate those.

101
00:05:57,240 --> 00:06:00,900
Now within the staph group
itself, the way that you

102
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:03,300
can tell the difference
between aureus and epidermidis

103
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:08,100
is going to be by a coagulase
test. So coagulase is a

104
00:06:08,100 --> 00:06:12,720
secreted enzyme that's
generated by staph aureus. It's

105
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:15,180
very close to clumping
factor, and it does basically

106
00:06:15,180 --> 00:06:18,190
the same thing. It will
recruit serum proteins like

107
00:06:18,190 --> 00:06:21,700
fibrin and platelets as
well to be able to coat the

108
00:06:21,700 --> 00:06:24,700
bacterial cells and shield
them from the immune response.

109
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:28,820
And so that is kind of a
unique virulence factor

110
00:06:28,820 --> 00:06:32,160
for staph aureus and
allows you to differentiate

111
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:35,520
it from epidermidis. And
you can see that test in

112
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:38,200
the tubes in the picture
down below. Whereas staph

113
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:42,740
epi, the blood serum
stays liquid versus staph

114
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:46,460
aureus, which creates a
clump with the blood serum.

115
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:51,220
Now, staph epi by itself
also has the ability

116
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:54,220
to cause endocarditis,
but usually is associated

117
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:58,060
with a subacute picture,
and it's also associated

118
00:06:58,060 --> 00:07:00,960
with prosthetic valves.
That really stems

119
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:03,540
from the type of capsule
that it makes that

120
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:06,620
allows it to adhere to
those types of substrates.

121
00:07:10,210 --> 00:07:14,560
that brings us to streptococcus
and the first example

122
00:07:14,560 --> 00:07:17,840
here is the strep viridens
group so that's a group

123
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:21,680
of streptococci that are
part of your normal oral

124
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:27,300
flora so species like strep
sanguinous mitus mutans

125
00:07:27,300 --> 00:07:30,220
among others now the way
to differentiate between

126
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:33,360
strep and staph like I
said is by using a catalase

127
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:36,700
test, and streps are going
to be catalase negative.

128
00:07:36,960 --> 00:07:41,660
And if you consider strep
as the causative agent

129
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:44,380
of IE, you want to start
thinking about some

130
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:47,220
sort of oral trauma,
some sort of dental

131
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:51,080
procedure that was done
that allowed for a portal

132
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:53,820
of entry for the bacteria
into the bloodstream.

133
00:07:54,460 --> 00:08:01,040
And when the viridin's
species cause IE, generally

134
00:08:01,040 --> 00:08:04,060
they are going to create
a subacute picture. And

135
00:08:04,060 --> 00:08:08,180
in fact, viridin's streps
are the most common

136
00:08:08,180 --> 00:08:13,400
cause of subacute IE. So
when they are attaching to

137
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:17,020
a heart valve or some
damaged endothelial cells,

138
00:08:17,020 --> 00:08:20,400
they're going to do that
by utilizing dextran

139
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:24,300
secretion. So in the same
way dextrans are going to

140
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:27,640
mediate the creation of
plaque biofilm in the mouth,

141
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:30,300
that's the same way that
they could attach to a

142
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,040
damaged heart valve and
start to create a vegetation

143
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:39,040
because of the adherence
mediated by dextran.

144
00:08:40,280 --> 00:08:45,260
Now, the other example is
Streptococcus galliliticus.

145
00:08:45,260 --> 00:08:48,580
Now, that used to be
called Streptococcus bovis.

146
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:50,020
Same thing,

147
00:08:50,580 --> 00:08:54,660
same type of bacterium,
just a different name. But

148
00:08:54,660 --> 00:08:57,880
this is not part of
the oral flora. So this

149
00:08:57,880 --> 00:09:02,020
specifically is an inhabitant
of the GI tract. And so

150
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:05,340
if you see strep galliiticus
causing an infection

151
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:09,340
in the heart, it has to
have come from the GI

152
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:12,700
tract. And so there is a
strong association with

153
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:17,040
other pathology in the
colon. So if that's the cause

154
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:19,360
and that's identified,
you would certainly want

155
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:22,480
to follow up with a
colonoscopy for your patient.

156
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:29,020
Related to strep is going
to be the enterococcus

157
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:31,340
group. And so you're
going to find some of the

158
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:34,960
same basic features as you
would see in strep. All

159
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:36,940
right, so you're going to
have gram-positive cocci.

160
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:40,280
They're going to be
arranged in short chains.

161
00:09:40,500 --> 00:09:44,180
It's also catalase negative,
which allows you to

162
00:09:44,180 --> 00:09:46,620
differentiate it from
the staph group as well.

163
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:50,420
Now, endococcus is going to
be a little bit more aligned

164
00:09:50,560 --> 00:09:56,140
with strep galliliticus
insofar as it is a gut

165
00:09:56,140 --> 00:10:01,060
bacterial species, in addition
to being found in the GU

166
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:06,420
tract. So you could then
differentiate your enterococcus

167
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:11,180
and strep galliticus
examples from the viridens

168
00:10:11,180 --> 00:10:15,380
group by running a bile
escalon test. And you can see

169
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:19,720
that in the picture on the
slide. So as escalon is

170
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:22,360
hydrolyzed, it will change
the medium from kind of an

171
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:26,340
orangey yellow color to a black
color which would indicate

172
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:30,100
a positive test now of
course you can further

173
00:10:30,100 --> 00:10:35,700
subdivide enterococcus and
strep galliiticus by either

174
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:39,740
running a pyr test or growing
in a high salt environment

175
00:10:39,740 --> 00:10:43,880
and both of those enterococcus
would be positive for

176
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:51,220
So that leaves us with the
atypical causes of IEE,

177
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:54,800
otherwise known as the
culture negative microbes.

178
00:10:55,030 --> 00:10:57,940
And that could be the
case because of a number

179
00:10:57,940 --> 00:11:00,860
of different reasons.
Either the microbes

180
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:04,940
are fastidious, or
they're intracellular, or

181
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:08,500
they just take a long
time to grow on a medium.

182
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:11,240
So we could loosely
categorize them

183
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:13,500
into gram-negative
and gram-positive.

184
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:17,280
From the gram-negative
category, you could pick

185
00:11:17,280 --> 00:11:19,700
out the Hossack group,
right? So that's responsible

186
00:11:19,700 --> 00:11:23,300
for somewhere in the order
of about 3% of all IE.

187
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:27,460
It is also part of the
normal oral flora for

188
00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:31,460
people. So you could think
about that basically in

189
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:33,920
terms of the way that
you think about the strep

190
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:36,920
viridens group, right? So
same portal of entry and

191
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:39,460
access to the bloodstream.
The only difference

192
00:11:39,460 --> 00:11:42,320
would be that the Hossack
group would take a long

193
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:46,800
time to grow on media
versus the Viridin's group

194
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,100
that would grow in, you
know, 24 to 48 hours.

195
00:11:50,890 --> 00:11:53,460
Coxiella burnetii, which
happens to be the most

196
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:57,420
common cause of culture
negative, i.e., happens to be

197
00:11:57,420 --> 00:12:00,580
difficult because it is
an intracellular microbe,

198
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:02,240
right? Or if we take a look

199
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:04,540
at the gram positive category,

200
00:12:04,780 --> 00:12:08,120
NVS, right? So NVS is
very similar in scope

201
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:10,960
to the strep viridin's
group, but happens

202
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:14,460
to need to be supplemented
with vitamin B6.

203
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:18,160
And of course, there's the
fungal causes, the most

204
00:12:18,160 --> 00:12:21,520
common of which would be
candida and aspergillus.

205
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:29,080
So what would we expect to
see in a patient with IE?

206
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:33,100
Well, first, there should
be a heart murmur, right?

207
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:36,400
So that would be brought
about by a vegetation

208
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:39,180
that's now disrupting the
functioning of the valve.

209
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:42,640
But you would also expect
there to be some systemic

210
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:46,580
symptoms due to just
generalized inflammation, and

211
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:49,760
that's where you would
see symptoms like a fever,

212
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:54,880
which most individuals
with IE would display, in

213
00:12:54,880 --> 00:12:59,840
addition to things like chills
or malaise, fatigue, weight

214
00:12:59,840 --> 00:13:03,910
loss, things like that. You
might also see petechiae,

215
00:13:03,910 --> 00:13:06,560
right, based on the
damage to the small blood

216
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:10,480
vessels in the capillaries
due to the inflammation.

217
00:13:10,980 --> 00:13:15,140
Now, the other things
listed there are going to be

218
00:13:15,140 --> 00:13:18,480
very much connected to
IE, although you wouldn't

219
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:21,760
necessarily see them
within patients all that

220
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:26,500
frequently. So the first is
Roth spots, which are retinal

221
00:13:26,500 --> 00:13:29,680
hemorrhages that have a
particular clearing in

222
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:33,680
the middle of them, brought
about by septic emboli.

223
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:38,220
Likewise, Janeway
lesions and splinter

224
00:13:38,220 --> 00:13:40,360
hemorrhages also
of the nail bed are

225
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:43,120
brought about by
septic emboli as well.

226
00:13:43,420 --> 00:13:46,500
And of course, then there's
the Osler's nodes, which

227
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:52,200
are kind of very tender,
painful, swelling or lesions

228
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:54,360
that you would find on
the fingers and the toes.

229
00:13:54,360 --> 00:13:56,620
Now, those are not
necessarily brought about

230
00:13:56,620 --> 00:14:00,760
by septic emboli, but
instead are due to

231
00:14:00,760 --> 00:14:03,880
a hypersensitivity
reaction and the deposition

232
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:06,980
of immune complexes
at those locations.

233
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:14,880
Now, as a reminder, the
system of diagnosis of IE

234
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:19,700
follows the modified major
and minor DUKE criteria, and

235
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:22,800
the combinations of those
